# Recent activity data sources

## Why

Three indexer endpoints provide pre-sorted, paginated feeds:

- `GET /api/v1/blocks` → `useLatestBlocks`
- `GET /api/v1/transactions/latest` → `useLatestTransactions`
- `GET /api/v1/transfers/latest` → `useLatestTransfers`

All three use cursor-based pagination via TanStack Query's `useInfiniteQuery`. Transfers can be filtered by `eventType[]` (`VET`, `FUNGIBLE_TOKEN`, `NFT`, `SEMI_FUNGIBLE_TOKEN`).

`/api/v1/blocks` serves collapsed headers only, and omits `isTrunk` and `isFinalized` — both are node-local and time-varying, so the index cannot serve them. Read those from Thor's `GET /blocks/{revision}`, which is also the only way to look a block up by id.

## Architecture

```
useLatestBlocks({ size })                   ← /api/v1/blocks
  ├─ useLatestBlocksLive → Homepage Blocks card   ← size=5, polls each block interval
  └─ /activity/blocks page                        ← cursor pagination

useLatestTransactions({ size, expanded })   ← /api/v1/transactions/latest
  ├─ Homepage Transactions card             ← size=5, expanded=false
  └─ /activity/transactions page            ← cursor pagination

useLatestTransfers({ size, eventType })     ← /api/v1/transfers/latest
  ├─ Homepage Token Transfers card          ← size=5, no filter (all event types)
  └─ /transfers page                        ← cursor pagination + filter chips
```

## Cursor pagination pattern

`useInfiniteQuery` returns `data.pages: PageResponse[]` where each page has `data` and `pagination.cursor`. To wrap this in classic numeric pagination:

```tsx
const { data, hasNextPage, fetchNextPage, isFetchingNextPage } = useLatestTransfers({ size: pageSize })
const pages = data?.pages ?? []
const currentPageData = pages[page]?.data ?? []
const hasNext = page < pages.length - 1 || Boolean(hasNextPage)

const handlePageChange = async (newPage: number) => {
  if (newPage > pages.length - 1 && hasNextPage && !isFetchingNextPage) {
    await fetchNextPage()
  }
  setPage(newPage)
}
```

Going forward fetches a new cursor; going back is free (already cached). Changing `pageSize` or `eventType` resets the query (different `queryKey`). For blocks the cursor is a block number, passed back as `from`.

## Per-block clause and VTHO totals

`/activity/blocks` shows clauses per block and VTHO paid, and both come off the block itself —
`clauseCount` and `totalVthoPaid`, added by vechain-indexer#1550. They are required fields, so a
network whose indexer has not shipped that change fails `indexerBlockSchema`.

Thor is not involved in either block list. Reach for it only for what the index genuinely cannot
serve: `isTrunk` / `isFinalized`, and lookup of a single block by id.
